# Goblin kit — the POLYGON half of the goblin, authored in WAM.
#
# WHY TWO LANGUAGES FOR ONE CHARACTER. .blob builds flesh out of blended
# signed-distance primitives, and it is very good at exactly the things a mesh
# is bad at: everything merges by construction, nothing has a seam, and a limb
# is four numbers. It is correspondingly bad at anything HARD — smooth-min
# rounds every edge it touches, so a buckle, a rivet, a plate lip or a blade
# either smears or has to be carved back with a `hard` blend that fights the
# whole aesthetic. WAM is a text→glTF mesh compiler, which is the other half of
# that trade. So: flesh in .blob, plate in .wam.
#
# The polygon layer also sidesteps a problem the SDF path has not solved. All
# the flesh shares ONE material — per-part colour needs a data-texture row and
# a second weighted fold at every hit pixel to avoid Voronoi seams mid-limb.
# A separate mesh just has its own material, so steel on green flesh is free.
#
# THE SKELETON BELOW MIRRORS characters/goblin.blob AND MUST TRACK IT. Every
# bone, angle and length is the same joint in the same place; if the .blob
# skeleton moves and this does not, the plate floats. Two conversions apply
# and neither is optional:
#
#   * UNITS. WAM lengths are fractions of `height`; .blob's are metres. Every
#     number here is the .blob metre value / 1.30.
#   * PITCH SIGN ON `down` BONES. WAM's pitch tips an `up` bone forward and a
#     `down` bone BACKWARD; .blob's always carries toward +z. So `up` bones
#     (spine1, chest, neck, skull) share their pitch verbatim, and every `down`
#     bone (upperarm, forearm, thigh, shin) has it NEGATED. Getting this wrong
#     is what gave the .blob goblin kangaroo knees in the first place, and it
#     is silent — the model compiles either way and just wears its armour on
#     the wrong side of the limb. `tilt` needs no such flip: both languages
#     splay a `down` bone outward on +.
#
# RING SIZES ARE MEASURED, NOT EYEBALLED. Each piece wraps flesh that is not
# in this file, so every w/d below comes from marching the .blob field outward
# from the bone axis, per limb-isolated cluster, and converting to height
# fractions — the SDF answers "where is the surface" directly, which is the
# one thing a mesh pipeline has to raycast for. Measured flesh, for reference:
#
#   upperarm t0.00 w=0.085  t0.18 w=0.055  t0.42 w=0.051
#   forearm  w=0.045 all along
#   hips     t0.40 w=0.109 d=0.146   t0.62 w=0.126 d=0.174   t0.90 w=0.134 d=0.184
#   shin     w=0.055 all along
#
# Every ring here sits a few thousandths proud of those. Re-measure before
# retuning: the arm-clearance work already moved the torso's whole cross
# section once.
# BUILD:  scripts/build-wam-kit.sh goblin
#   -> public/assets/lab/goblin-kit.gltf, which the lab loads (KITS in
#      lab-main.ts). WAM lives outside this repo, so the compiled glTF is
#      committed and that script records how to regenerate it.
#
# THREE OF WAM'S LINTS ALWAYS FIRE ON A KIT AND ARE CORRECT TO IGNORE. They
# check a whole CHARACTER, and this is only the clothes: `spine1`/`neck`/`skull`
# have no geometry near them (that flesh lives in the .blob), and the lowest
# geometry floats 0.174 above the ground (there are no feet here). A warning
# about anything else is real.
model goblin-kit
  height 1.30
  # chunky = 8-sided lofts. Faceted plate against smooth raymarched flesh is
  # the contrast this experiment is for — if it reads as cheap rather than
  # deliberate, this is the first knob to try at `smooth`.
  style chunky

palette
  iron    #6e7378
  leather #4a3526
  brass   #9c7a38

skeleton
  # `hips` HAS NO COUNTERPART IN goblin.blob, and leaving it out is a silent
  # 10.5 cm error. .blob's root is a BONE with a length — `root pelvis at 0.60
  # len=0.105` runs from the crotch up to the hip line, and everything that
  # says `parent=pelvis` there hangs off its TAIL. WAM's root is a bare POINT,
  # so parenting spine1 straight to it drops the entire trunk (and every piece
  # of plate on it) by that length. `hips` restores the missing segment, and
  # every .blob `parent=pelvis` becomes `parent=hips` here.
  #
  # It also carries the belt: `bones=pelvis..pelvis` is a zero-length path and
  # WAM rejects it outright, which is how this was found.
  root pelvis at 0.4615
  bone hips   parent=pelvis dir=up len=0.0808
  bone spine1 parent=hips dir=up pitch=13 len=0.1038
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=9  len=0.1154
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-17 len=0.1077
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-5  len=0.1808
  mirror
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.0277 dir=side len=0.0323
    # pitch NEGATED from .blob's -12 — see the header.
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=12  tilt=22 len=0.1808
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=-11 tilt=-5 len=0.1808
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down len=0.0554
    bone thigh    parent=hips     side=0.0446 dir=down pitch=-9 tilt=5  len=0.2231
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down pitch=11  tilt=-7 len=0.2077
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd len=0.1000
  end

parts
  # EVERY PIECE IS AN OPEN SHELL — `cap ... none` almost throughout, and that
  # is a correctness decision, not a shortcut. A `cap flat` on a tube that
  # wraps a limb puts a disc THROUGH the limb, with a vertex dead on the bone
  # axis: the belt's cap centre sat 61 mm inside the goblin, the greaves' 35 mm.
  # Hidden while the flesh happens to be thick enough to swallow them, and a
  # plate poking out of a shin the moment it is not. Real plate is a shell
  # anyway, so the geometry is also more honest. Rendered double-sided by
  # kit-overlay.ts, which is what an open shell needs.
  #
  # It has a second payoff: with no interior geometry, EVERY vertex is on the
  # outer surface, so "no vertex may sit inside the flesh" becomes an exact
  # test rather than one that has to guess which vertices are hidden. That is
  # goblin-kit.test.ts, and it reads the compiled mesh, not this text.
  mirror
    # PAULDRON — a spherical SHELL over the shoulder orb, continuing into a
    # flared skirt down the upper arm.
    #
    # Not a dome cap on a tube, which is what this was and what the owner
    # rejected: the orb punched straight through the plate. .blob's shoulder is
    # a sphere of radius 0.054 m centred on the clavicle tail, reaching y=1.039,
    # and the plate crowned at 1.020. WAM's `cap dome` is a SHALLOW cap — it
    # extends about 0.44 of the ring's radius past the last ring, measured —
    # so cresting the orb that way needs a ring 0.30 m across on a 1.30 m
    # goblin. The shell is built explicitly instead: a free ray aimed along the
    # arm, starting 0.070 m ABOVE the orb (a bone loft cannot start at t<0), with
    # every ring set to the sphere's own cross-section at that distance —
    # sqrt(R² - s²) for R = orb + 8 mm clearance — so the plate rides the orb
    # instead of cutting through it. Past the equator it stops following the
    # sphere and flares over the arm.
    #
    # The offset is 0.070 m back along the arm's unit direction
    # (0.366, -0.906, -0.209), in height fractions. `pitch=12 tilt=22` re-aims
    # the ray along that same arm axis — NOTE the pitch sign is WAM's, the
    # negation of .blob's -12 (see the header).
    #
    # ITS INNER RIM IS BURIED IN THE CHEST, and that is forced, not sloppy. The
    # orb is 108 mm across and sits 78 mm from the body axis, so ANY ring that
    # encloses it reaches past the centreline; there is no shell that both
    # covers the orb and clears the ribs. Real pauldrons tuck under the same
    # way. goblin-kit.test.ts allows the tuck and bounds it.
    loft pauldron bone=upperarm at=0.0 offset=(-0.0197,0.0487,0.0112) dir=down pitch=12 tilt=22 len=0.200 material=iron
      ring 0.05 w=0.038 d=0.036
      ring 0.15 w=0.083 d=0.080
      ring 0.27 w=0.095 d=0.092
      ring 0.40 w=0.080 d=0.078
      ring 0.58 w=0.092 d=0.088
      ring 0.78 w=0.086 d=0.082
      cap start=dome end=none

    # BRACER — a leather cuff. Flesh is 0.045 the whole way down the forearm.
    loft bracer bones=forearm..forearm material=leather
      ring 0.34 w=0.060 d=0.060
      ring 0.58 w=0.066 d=0.066
      ring 0.84 w=0.062 d=0.062
      cap start=none end=none

    # GREAVE — the same idea down the shin, stopping above the ankle.
    loft greave bones=shin..shin material=iron
      ring 0.14 w=0.078 d=0.078
      ring 0.45 w=0.076 d=0.076
      ring 0.72 w=0.070 d=0.070
      cap start=none end=none

    # BOOT — pointed, and lofted along a FREE RAY rather than the foot bone,
    # because the foot's toe blob (`at=0.92`, deep=0.95) bulges past the bone's
    # own tail. A boot ending at t=1.0 of the foot bone stops short of the toe
    # it is supposed to cover. The ray is the foot bone continued: same origin
    # (shin tail), same `dir=fwd`, a third longer.
    loft boot bone=shin at=1.0 dir=fwd len=0.168 frame=up material=leather
      ring 0.03 w=0.086 d=0.084
      ring 0.28 w=0.076 d=0.062
      ring 0.55 w=0.080 d=0.056
      ring 0.78 w=0.062 d=0.040
      ring 0.92 w=0.034 d=0.026
      ring 1.00 w=0.012 d=0.010 tip
      cap start=none end=point
  end

  # BREASTPLATE — an open shell around the ribcage, from just under the collar
  # to the bottom of the chest bone. It stops there deliberately: the gut below
  # is the goblin's silhouette and armouring it would square off the one shape
  # that reads as a creature rather than a soldier.
  loft breastplate bones=chest..chest material=iron frame=fwd
    ring 0.16 w=0.100 d=0.118
    ring 0.44 w=0.124 d=0.140
    ring 0.74 w=0.120 d=0.136
    ring 0.96 w=0.108 d=0.112
    cap start=none end=none

  # BELT — around the pelvis, above the widest part of the gut. Deliberately
  # not at the fattest ring: a belt there reads as a stripe painted on the
  # stomach, whereas one above it reads as something the stomach hangs over,
  # which is the goblin note.
  loft belt bones=hips..hips material=leather
    ring 0.32 w=0.122 d=0.156
    ring 0.54 w=0.134 d=0.182
    ring 0.68 w=0.140 d=0.190
    cap start=none end=none

  # BUCKLE — brass on the front of the belt. NOT `on=belt`: that snaps the
  # origin to the belt's surface and then sinks it in by an inset, which buried
  # this so far that even its FRONT face measured 1 mm inside the goblin. It is
  # placed by hand and checked by the fit test instead.
  attach buckle bone=hips kind=box at=0.52 offset=(0,0,0.135) size=0.030 w=0.044 d=0.020 material=brass

  # FAULD — a short flared skirt of plate hanging off the belt. This replaces a
  # cloth kilt that reached the knees; the owner's verdict was "way too long",
  # and it also clipped both thighs at the hem and the gut at the waist.
  #
  # Short is not just taste here. The thighs splay as they descend, so a skirt
  # that hangs any distance has to flare very wide to clear them — by the knee
  # it needs to be wider than the goblin's own belly. Stopping high keeps it
  # plate-shaped instead of tent-shaped.
  loft fauld bone=hips at=0.36 dir=down len=0.072 material=iron frame=fwd
    ring 0.00 w=0.128 d=0.162
    ring 0.55 w=0.158 d=0.196
    ring 1.00 w=0.186 d=0.222
    cap start=none end=none

  # CLEAVER — a compound prop in its own clean local frame: haft along the
  # group's +Y, blade above the grip. Aiming the GROUP is one number; rotating
  # a hand-derived world angle for each of two lofts is three, and they have to
  # agree. `dir=down pitch=-58` drops the blade forward of the fist.
  group cleaver bone=hand.r at=1.0 dir=up pitch=16 yaw=-20
    # BLADE-UP, HEAD NEAR THE FIST. Aimed `dir=down` with the blade at the far
    # end of the haft — which is the obvious way to author "held point down" —
    # this read as a SPADE from every angle: a long shaft with a flat paddle on
    # the bottom is a shovel, whatever you call the material. A chopper's mass
    # sits at the hand end. The haft now hangs BELOW the fist as a stub pommel
    # and the head rises just above it.
    #
    # `yaw=-20` swings the head outward. Without it the blade sweeps through
    # the fauld — kit-against-kit intersection, which nothing checks: the fit
    # test measures armour against FLESH, and two pieces of plate occupying the
    # same space are invisible to it.
    loft haft at=(0,-0.058,0) dir=up len=0.140 material=leather
      ring 0.00 w=0.024 d=0.024
      ring 1.00 w=0.019 d=0.019
      cap start=dome end=flat
    loft blade at=(0,0.048,0) dir=up len=0.112 material=iron shape=box
      # Tapering OUTWARD to the tip is what makes a cleaver read as a cleaver
      # rather than a paddle: mass at the far edge, thin at the haft.
      ring 0.00 w=0.038 d=0.014
      ring 0.32 w=0.070 d=0.012
      ring 0.74 w=0.090 d=0.010
      ring 1.00 w=0.094 d=0.007
      cap start=flat end=flat
  end

  # SHIELD — a dished disc on the off hand. `dir=side` points the group's own
  # +Y out along the arm, so the disc lies across it and the boss faces away
  # from the goblin.
  # SHIELD — `dir=fwd`, NOT `dir=side`. A group's aim points its local +Y, and
  # the disc is lofted along that +Y, so the aim IS the shield's face normal.
  # Pointed sideways the shield faces out along the arm and presents edge-on
  # from the front — a thin sliver that reads as a coin, which is what the
  # first version did. `yaw=-22` turns it slightly outward so it is not a flat
  # circle head-on either.
  group shield bone=hand.l at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=-22
    # Pushed forward along its own aim so the disc sits IN FRONT of the hip
    # rather than through the thigh. A shield this size cannot hang beside a
    # goblin's leg: the hand rests at y=0.47 with the thigh right behind it, so
    # anything wider than a 5 cm buckler cuts the leg. Standing it off is the
    # cheap fix; the real one is posing the arm, which needs the kit rigged.
    loft face at=(0,0.035,0) dir=up len=0.030 material=iron
      ring 0.00 w=0.146 d=0.146
      ring 0.60 w=0.168 d=0.168
      ring 1.00 w=0.132 d=0.132
      cap start=dome end=flat
    loft boss at=(0,0.065,0) dir=up len=0.030 material=brass
      ring 0.00 w=0.052 d=0.052
      ring 1.00 w=0.030 d=0.030
      cap start=flat end=dome
  end

textures
  texture iron base=#6e7378
    gradient axis=v from=-0.12 to=0.18   # painted top-light on the plate
    noise scale=0.10 amount=0.06         # hammered, not stamped
    ao amount=0.18
  texture leather base=#4a3526
    streaks along=v amount=0.09
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.08
    ao amount=0.20
  texture brass base=#9c7a38
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.22
    ao amount=0.12
